Three corrections to that review:
1. Its DO-duration argument is misleading. Current pricing: an object idle and ELIGIBLE
   for hibernation is not billed for duration even before the runtime hibernates it. The
   Coordinator has no WebSockets, no timers and no in-flight fetch, so it is always
   eligible when idle — its duration bill is ~the sum of RPC execution time. The real
   cost of 5s dashboard polling is DO REQUESTS (~518k/month/tab against 1M included),
   not duration. That is why the dashboard fix ranked first here, not third.
2. Its transactionSync item (18) is a non-issue: coordinator.ts contains zero `await`,
   and the SQLite API documents that a series of writes with no intervening await is
   submitted atomically.
3. Its discovery item (12) overstates the defect: scanRepo completes a repo before its
   batch resolves and the cursor only advances past a COMPLETED batch, so no repo is
   ever "partially scanned but counted as covered". The real behaviour is an overshoot
   of at most one batch, already documented and already warned via budgetBound.

Under-called by that review and still OPEN (not fixed here): shapes.ts:17 and
auth.ts:142 hand an in-flight promise created by one request to another request — the
pattern behind "Cannot perform I/O on behalf of a different request", which would fire
during exactly the matrix bursts this serves. Do not build a repro harness first: grep
production Workers Logs for "different request". The lazy fix is deletion — cache only
settled values (tokens last an hour, catalogs five minutes) and drop the in-flight
slots.

Deliberately NOT done: Queues (review item 8). Correctly diagnosed, but a large build
guarded by "no evidence this has happened". CREATE_BUDGET_MS makes the overrun loud and
measurable first; Queues (15-min consumer wall time) earns its keep only if measured
happy-path provisions crowd 22s. Also skipped: launchRunner's own 60s SDK timeout (the
measured path is sub-second — bounding it on a guess trades a known-good path for a
false failure), and review items 6, 13-17, 19-26.

Known risk in this commit: 22s is a ceiling chosen from the budget arithmetic, not a
measured p95. If real creates exceed it, those jobs now re-queue (~5 min) instead of
finishing at, say, 25s. The new `createSandbox budget spent after 22000ms` warn line is
the signal; raise the constant if it fires on healthy boots.

One fixture changed meaning: a restricted tenant with ZERO approved Projects now scans
nothing (previously its whole installation). The admin API already refuses to approve
such a tenant, so this state is fixture-only.

